<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Counter-Reformation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1545–<a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Catholic Reformation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was the period of Catholic renewal and response to the Protestant Reformation, from the mid-16th to the mid-17th century. Its programme was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> (1545–1563), which defined doctrine and reformed the clergy, and it was carried forward by new orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> and by Catholic rulers who restored their faith in their lands. It also shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art and architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counter-reformation.jpg" alt="Counter-Reformation" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolom%C3%A9_Esteban_Murillo,_Faith_or_The_Church_Triumphant_as,_165_x_251_cm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
